{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And you shall come to the priest who shall be in those days.</b> Since our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Ketubot 105a): Now was Elisha one who ate first-fruits? Rather, to teach you that anyone who brings a gift to a sage is as if he offers first-fruits — and since the bringing of a gift to a sage is compared to first-fruits, I would have thought that they should not give the first-fruits except to a priest who is a sage who happens to be in that watch, and if there is no sage among them, I would have thought that he should wait until the watch of some priest who is a sage arrives — Scripture comes to teach: 'who shall be in those days,' and by this the words of the Ramban are resolved, for he wrote 'and I did not understand this,' etc. 'And you shall say to him: I have declared' — already in this bringing, even though there is no speech and no words, in the manner that is said (Psalms 19:2–4): 'The firmament declares the work of His hands,' etc. — 'There is no speech and there are no words,' etc. — so too this bringing declares and testifies concerning me that I came to the land, not through my righteousness, but only because He kept the oath that HaShem swore to our fathers." "And since the declaration is an altar of atonement, therefore: 'And the priest shall take the basket from your hand and set it down before the altar of HaShem' — to say: behold, your iniquity has departed and your sin will be atoned. And after the atonement: 'And you shall answer and say' — in a loud voice, not like the confession that was in a whisper, for it is brazen for one who details his sins in public; but this matter he may say in a loud voice, to remove the transgression from the seed of Jacob and to hang it upon the corrupt one, upon the seed of the mother who was from Aram. And he says: 'An Aramean sought to destroy my father' — he did not mention Laban by name, but to say that because he was from Aram, therefore he conducted himself according to the way of his place, and from him was drawn this evil trait mentioned in the verse 'and you shall take possession of it and dwell in it' — to seek a dwelling of tranquility in this world, as is concluded in Bereshit Rabbah (parasha 39:8): Rabbi Levi said: At the time Abraham was journeying through Aram Naharaim and Aram Nahor and saw the people eating and drinking and acting recklessly, he said: Would that my portion not be in this land, etc. And since from there Isaac took Rebecca, and Jacob his wives, from there Jacob learned to seek a dwelling of tranquility in this world, and through this the wrath of Joseph's affair leaped upon him, and through this the matter turned about so that they went down to Egypt, and there they were sojourners and came into the iron crucible to purge that sin. And this is what is said: 'An Aramean sought to destroy my father, and he went down to Egypt.' And he mentioned this by way of exculpation for what they are accused of in the verse 'and you shall take possession of it and dwell in it,' as mentioned, to say that from this sin a trace remained somewhat for the generations; therefore he said 'sought to destroy (אֹבֵד) my father' — 'אֹבֵד' in the present tense, to say that this trace remained even for the generations."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:13:59.102061309Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}